# Quote: Mario Bunge - Treatise on Basic Philosophy - Epistemology & Methodology III - Part II

> All the schools of verbal psychotherapy, in particular the various psychoanalytic splinter groups, are dualistic, and neither of them relies on experiment: they are relics of prescientific medicine. Nearly one century after the inception of psychoanalysis, only about 500 outcome studies are available, only some 30 of which include a piacebo treatment. A careful review of the placebo controlled studies shows that "the benefits of psychotherapy do not exceed those obtained from placebo treatment" (Prioleau et al. 1983). Previous studies have also shown that experience in psychotherapy is irrelevant to therapeutic outcome (Durlak 1979), and that duration of treatment is equally irrelevant to size of therapeutic effect (Smith et al., 1980). Other studies have found that the type of therapy makes no difference either: about 40 % of neurotic patients improve after a few years regardless of therapeutic school - or even spontaneously. (See Garfield and Bergin Eds. 1978.)

